# Electron Development Guidelines

Directives for building secure, performant Electron applications. Security first; process separation; resource management. Advanced main-process patterns (window manager, typed IPC client, plugins, recovery) live in `advanced-patterns.mdc`.

## Process Model

- **Main process**: Node.js environment, system APIs, window creation, IPC handlers
- **Preload**: Runs before the page in an isolated world; bridges a minimal, typed API with `contextBridge`
- **Renderer process**: Chromium UI; sandboxed, no Node.js access
- **Utility process** (`utilityProcess.fork`): CPU-heavy or crash-prone work off the main process

Register handlers for `uncaughtException`, `unhandledRejection`, and `render-process-gone`; log and recover or shut down gracefully.

## Project Structure

```
src/
├── main/            # entry, windows/, ipc/ (handlers), services/
├── preload/         # preload scripts (one per window type if APIs differ)
├── renderer/        # UI (React/Vite)
└── shared/          # IPC channel names + request/response types, constants
```

- IPC handlers are thin: validate, then call a service
- Path aliases: `@main/*`, `@renderer/*`, `@shared/*`; separate tsconfigs for main/preload and renderer

## Security Fundamentals

Follow the Electron security checklist. Keep Electron on a supported major (latest three stable majors receive fixes).

### Web Preferences

```typescript
new BrowserWindow({
  webPreferences: {
    preload: path.join(__dirname, '../preload/index.js'),
    contextIsolation: true,        // default; never disable
    sandbox: true,                 // default since Electron 20; never disable
    nodeIntegration: false,        // default; never enable
    webSecurity: true,             // never disable
    allowRunningInsecureContent: false,
  },
});
```

- Sandboxed preloads can only `require` a limited set of modules (`electron`'s `contextBridge`, `ipcRenderer`, etc.); do file system work in main
- Never expose `ipcRenderer`, `process`, `require`, or `fs` through `contextBridge` — expose one function per operation

```typescript
// preload
contextBridge.exposeInMainWorld('api', {
  saveFile: (data: string) => ipcRenderer.invoke('file:save', data),
});
```

### Content Security Policy

- Serve a strict CSP (`<meta http-equiv>` for `file://`/custom protocol, `onHeadersReceived` for HTTP): `default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'`
- No `'unsafe-eval'`; avoid `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts
- Load local content through a custom protocol (`protocol.handle('app', ...)`) rather than `file://`

### Navigation and New Windows

```typescript
app.on('web-contents-created', (_e, contents) => {
  contents.setWindowOpenHandler(({ url }) => {
    if (isAllowedExternal(url)) void shell.openExternal(url);
    return { action: 'deny' };
  });
  contents.on('will-navigate', (event, url) => {
    if (new URL(url).origin !== APP_ORIGIN) event.preventDefault();
  });
});
```

- `shell.openExternal` only with validated `https:` URLs from an allow-list — never with raw renderer input
- Handle `session.setPermissionRequestHandler` and deny by default
- Do not use `<webview>`; prefer `WebContentsView`, and validate its options in `will-attach-webview` if unavoidable
- Enable Electron Fuses at package time (`RunAsNode: false`, `EnableNodeOptionsEnvironmentVariable: false`, `EnableEmbeddedAsarIntegrityValidation: true`, `OnlyLoadAppFromAsar: true`)

## IPC Communication

- Channel names and payload types live in `shared/ipc`; use `module:action` naming
- Use `ipcMain.handle` / `ipcRenderer.invoke` for request–response; reserve `send`/`on` for events
- Validate the **sender** on every handler: check `event.senderFrame` origin, not just `isMainFrame`
- Validate every **payload** with a schema (Zod) — type, size, and format

```typescript
ipcMain.handle('file:save', async (event, raw) => {
  assertTrustedSender(event);                     // event.senderFrame?.url origin === APP_ORIGIN
  const data = SaveFileRequest.parse(raw);        // zod: z.string().max(1_000_000)
  return fileService.save(data);
});
```

## Window and Resource Management

- Create windows through one module; apply the same `webPreferences` to every window
- On `closed`, drop references; remove IPC listeners and timers registered for that window
- Persist and restore bounds; clamp restored bounds to visible displays

## Performance

- Keep the main process responsive: no synchronous I/O or heavy computation (use `utilityProcess` or worker threads)
- Keep preload minimal; lazy-load heavy renderer modules and code-split routes
- Batch high-frequency IPC; transfer large binary data with `MessagePort` or `ArrayBuffer`
- Show windows on `ready-to-show` to avoid white flashes

## Common Anti-patterns

- `nodeIntegration: true`, `contextIsolation: false`, `sandbox: false`, or `webSecurity: false`
- Exposing raw `ipcRenderer` or Node APIs in preload
- `shell.openExternal(url)` with unvalidated input; missing `setWindowOpenHandler`/`will-navigate` guards
- IPC handlers that trust the payload or the sender
- Using the removed `remote` module (or `@electron/remote`) — move logic to main behind IPC
- `executeJavaScript` with unsanitised data

## Checklist

- [ ] Context isolation, sandbox, and web security enabled; node integration disabled
- [ ] Preload exposes a minimal, typed API via `contextBridge`
- [ ] CSP applied; local content served via custom protocol
- [ ] Navigation and new-window handlers deny by default; `openExternal` URLs allow-listed
- [ ] Every IPC handler validates sender and payload
- [ ] Permission requests denied by default; fuses configured
- [ ] Windows and listeners cleaned up on close; main process never blocked
